        {
          "type": "answer",
          "text": "Great thinking! Implicit (unstated) premises are assumptions that the speaker takes for granted. We leave them out all the time because stating every single assumption would make arguments unbearably long and tedious. But it's important to identify implicit premises because they're often the weakest part of an argument! If the implicit premise is false or doubtful, the whole argument falls apart. Arguments with implicit premises are called 'enthymemes.'"
        }
