          "text": "Perfect! Independent support means each premise gives separate reasons for the conclusion — if you remove one, the other still provides some support. Linked support means premises work together — if you remove one, the others provide little or no support. This matters for evaluation: with independent premises, the argument can still be strong even if one premise fails. With linked premises, if any single premise fails, the whole argument collapses. In business analysis, this helps you know where to focus your critique. If a key business case relies on linked premises ('large TAM AND high margins AND low churn = great investment'), then challenging just one of those can undermine the whole case.",
