          "type": "answer",
          "text": "Great thinking! A good argument, from a critical thinking perspective, is one that satisfies relevant intellectual standards. The two most fundamental are: (1) Logical correctness — is the reasoning valid (for deduction) or strong (for induction)? (2) Accuracy — are all the premises true? But there are MORE standards: clarity, precision, relevance, consistency, completeness, and fairness. These matter because an argument could be logically correct with true premises and still be badly expressed, incomplete, one-sided, or ambiguous. In business, 'good argument' is often confused with 'persuasive presentation' — but a polished deck and confident delivery don't make the reasoning good.",
          "hint": ""
