        {
          "type": "answer",
          "text": "Excellent! The principle of rational acceptance says: generally, it's reasonable to accept a claim if three conditions are met: (1) The claim does NOT conflict with your personal experiences that you have no good reason to doubt. (2) The claim does NOT conflict with your background beliefs that are well-supported. (3) The claim comes from a credible source. If all three hold, it's reasonable to accept the claim (at least provisionally). If any of these FAIL, then you should be skeptical and demand more evidence. In business, this principle helps you triage claims: 'Our churn is 5% per month' (from a reliable internal report) — accept it. 'This new technology will revolutionize our industry' (from a salesperson with a quota) — be very skeptical.",
          "hint": ""
        }
