      description: 'In 2019, WeWork—then valued at $47 billion and widely hailed as a revolutionary real estate company—filed its S-1 registration statement in preparation for an initial public offering. The company\'s pitch to investors was bold: WeWork was not just an office rental company but a "space-as-a-service" platform that would transform the way people work, live, and learn. CEO Adam Neumann argued that WeWork\'s "community-adjusted EBITDA" metric proved profitability, that its rapid growth demonstrated massive market demand, and that its technology platform and brand moat would sustain its dominance for decades to come.\n\nHowever, as analysts and journalists began examining the S-1 filing, serious questions emerged. The company had lost $1.9 billion in 2018 despite $1.8 billion in revenue. Its "community-adjusted EBITDA" excluded basic operating expenses like rent and marketing. Its lease obligations exceeded $47 billion in long-term liabilities. Related-party transactions with Neumann himself raised governance red flags. The IPO ultimately collapsed, Neumann was ousted, and the company\'s valuation plummeted.\n\nYour task is to analyze WeWork\'s core investment argument as presented in its S-1 and public communications. You will break down the company\'s case for its $47 billion valuation into its logical components, identify the structure of premises and conclusions, detect missing premises and assumptions, and evaluate the overall strength of the argument\'s structure. This is not primarily an exercise in hindsight judgment—it is an exercise in reconstructing and auditing the logical architecture of a business argument as it was presented at the time.',
