    {
      "id": "ch2_post_004",
      "author": "Student E",
      "authorRole": "student",
      "isPinned": false,
      "isFeatured": false,
      "title": "How do you distinguish an illustration from an argument?",
      "content": "I'm struggling with the difference between an illustration (giving an example of a general point) and an argument (giving reasons for a conclusion).\n\nIn the marketing context, when they show someone using the product and looking happy — is that an illustration of what the product does? Or is it an argument that you should buy it?\n\nMaybe both? How do you tell the difference?",
      "category": "Help",
      "timestamp": 1787204388049,
      "likes": 4,
      "likedByMe": false,
      "replies": [
        {
          "id": "r2_4_1",
          "author": "AI Instructor",
          "authorRole": "instructor",
          "content": "Great question! Here's the key test:\n\nAn illustration's purpose is to CLARIFY or GIVE AN EXAMPLE of a statement that's already accepted. Example: 'Critical thinking is important. For instance, it helps you make better decisions.' The 'for instance' part is an illustration — it's not trying to PROVE critical thinking is important, just to show what it looks like.\n\nAn argument's purpose is to PROVE or PERSUADE someone of a claim they might not already accept. Example: 'You should study critical thinking because it will help you make better decisions and avoid being manipulated.' The 'because' part is a premise supporting the conclusion.\n\nIn marketing, the happy customer is usually PART of an argument: 'You should buy this because it will make you happy too.' It's not just illustrating — it's persuading. The test is always: is the speaker trying to get you to accept something new (argument), or just explaining/showing something already agreed on (illustration)?",
          "timestamp": 1787290788049
        }
      ]
    }
